00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.860
Now, let us turn to a strategy that has historically thrived

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.880
in exactly this kind of selective rotating market.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:11.360
Quality compounding focuses on identifying

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.440
durable businesses trading below and intrinsic value

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:17.480
using metrics such as return on invested capital

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.820
and free cash flow yield rather than headline growth.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.300
Today's environment of selective AI rebounds

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.560
and broader market rotation makes this approach relevant

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:31.900
because expensive momentum names can mask undervalued compounders

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.480
that deliver steadier long-term results.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.120
Implement it by screening for companies

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.900
with consistent earnings growth above 8%

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:44.200
and enterprise value to EEbit below sector medians.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.620
Then cross-check against 10-year average multiples

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:49.680
on platforms such as trading view

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.760
or your broker's fundamental scanner.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:56.160
This strategy performed best during 2018 to 2019

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.620
and 2022 rate hike periods when growth multiples compressed

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:02.720
and value re-rated upward.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.700
The primary risk is opportunity cost

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.540
during strong momentum regimes.

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:13.220
So limit position size to 5% and maintain a 12 to 24 month horizon.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:16.460
If you want to try this with your TFSA,

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:18.480
start by pulling a list of names

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:20.860
that meet the growth and valuation filters.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.180
Then rank them by free cash flow yield before allocating.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:28.860
Now, here's something that most retail investors get wrong

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.340
and it cost me money before I figured it out.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:36.800
Imagine you added a 3% crypto sleeve to your TFSA last month

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:40.080
when Bitcoin broke above its 200 day moving average.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.880
Your order filled at the quoted price

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:48.440
but the exchange spread widened from 0.2% to 0.8%

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.340
during the first hour of volatility.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:54.240
An on-chain settlement added another 15 minute delay

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.420
before your wallet reflected the position.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.320
Canadian listed products such as BTCX and ETX

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.340
already embed custody and regulatory overhead.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:07.800
So the true cost difference versus self-custody

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:13.100
is often only the 0.5 to 1.0% annual management fee

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:16.160
versus exchange hacking or key loss risk.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:18.940
The biggest mistake with crypto allocation

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:21.060
is treating it like a momentum trade

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.640
instead of a non-correlated diversifier.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:26.340
Instead, always size the position

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:30.480
so a 50% drawdown still leaves total portfolio volatility

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.920
under your target and rebalance only once per quarter.

